Mauro,

(Copying the blavaan list, in case this is helpful for somebody else...)

1. Regarding the nu prior, I don't have a specific opinion about the precision of .25: I don't know the data or the previous results of similar data. You might consider a sensitivity analysis where you vary the priors and see how results change.

2 & 3. This info can be found in the documentation:

?dpriors

Ed

On Tue, Jan 31, 2017 at 5:48 AM, Gian Mauro Manzoni <gianmaur...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Dear Ed,
> I really need help for the specification of priors in the bcfa command. Please consider the following syntax.
>
> model <- 'abnormal emotional regulation =~ q1+q2+q3+q4+q5+q6
>                 food seeking =~ q7+q8+q9+q10+q11+q12+q13+q14+q15+q16
>                 lack of flexibility =~ q17+q18+q19+q20+q21+q22+q23+q24
>                 oppositional behavior =~ q25+q26+q27+q28+q29+q30+q31+q32
>                 body related =~ q33+q34+q35+q36+q37'
>
>
> bfit <- bcfa(model, data = PWS, convergence = "auto",
>                    dp = dpriors (itheta = "dunif(0,2)[sd]",  ## uniform priors on the items' standard deviations (Likert response scale with five levels, i.e. 0-1-2-3-4)
>                                          nu = "dnorm(2,.25)T(0,4)", ## prior distributions (truncated normals) on the items' intercept parameters
>                                          lambda = "dunif(0,3)", ## prior distribution on the factor loadings
>                                          ??     )
>
> 1 - Should I change the 0.25 specification in the nu prior given the response scale of my items?
> 2 - What are the parameter classes for specifying the priors of factor variances and covariances?
> 3 - What is the the parameter class for specifying the prior of residual variances?
>
>
> Thank you very much!!!
>
> Sincerely,
> Mauro

